+
+## Design Notes (boundaries the implementation must respect)
+
+1. **Extraction point**: `classify_one_key` already receives the matched `EventEnvelope` and only returns `Present(sequence)` today. Widen the presence result so that when the expected decision kind is `MutationDecisionKind::SignalRecorded` and the matching event is `EventKind::SignalRecorded(payload)`, the classifier captures `payload.signal_id.as_str()`. For every other decision kind the captured value is `None`.
+2. **Carrying**: `KeyPresence::Present` and `KeyState::Complete` gain the optional identifier, captured alongside `original_decision_sequence` from the first matched decision key (the same event whose sequence is already reported). No second history scan; no extra store open.
+3. **Annotation**: `annotate_recognized_retry` inserts `"signalId"` into the `data.idempotency` object only when the carried value is `Some`. Because only `execution.signal` maps to `SignalRecorded` in `MutationDecisionKind::for_command`, every other mutation command structurally keeps today's two-field proof (FR-007), and refusals (`Divergent`, `Partial`) never reach `annotate_recognized_retry` at all (FR-004/FR-005).
+4. **Fresh submissions**: the fresh-success path builds its own bespoke `data` (including `signalId`) in `execution::signal::execute` and never calls the annotation; unchanged (FR-006). Plain `GET /v1/executions/{id}` uses `execution::status::execute` directly with no proof; unchanged (FR-003).
+5. **Both `Complete` call sites** (pre-flight at the top of `run_idempotent_mutation_inner` and the post-append conflict reclassification) feed `reply_with_current_status` identically — both automatically report the identifier.
+6. **Field naming**: `signalId`, camelCase inside the existing proof object, mirroring the fresh reply's `data.signalId` and the input request's wording.
