+# Research: Signal Identity on Recognized Retries (001-bench)
+
+**Date**: 2026-10-26 · **Phase 0 output** of `__SPECKIT_COMMAND_PLAN__`
+
+Method note: the Technical Context contained no open `NEEDS CLARIFICATION` items (the spec resolved every ambiguity as a recorded informed default under its Assumptions). Research was therefore done by direct inspection of this checkout — the single source of truth for the task — at HEAD `a2bf7d9`. Findings below are file-and-line verified in this tree.
+
+## R1 — Where the original `signalId` comes from on a recognized retry
+
+**Decision**: Read it from the **committed `signal_recorded` event payload** (`SignalRecorded.signal_id`, `core/protocols/src/event.rs:965-973`) at the moment the key classifier already matches that event. Never from the current status snapshot, never from the request body, never recomputed.
+
+**Rationale**:
+- The classifier (`ExpectedDecision::matches` → `MutationDecisionKind::matches`, `apps/cli/src/commands/serve/mod.rs:761-788`) already verifies, for a recognized signal retry: same actor, scope/stream/execution, decision kind `signal_recorded`, `payload.signal_id == request.signal.id`, `source_id`, `source_kind`, `kind`, `severity`, **and** `sha256(canonical request.signal bytes) == payload.envelope_sha256`. So at the point of `KeyPresence::Present`, the stored `signal_id` is proven identical to what the caller sent — reading the durable record is both authoritative and free. This matches the spec's assumption that "the decision-kind match verifies the envelope's identity against the stored record" and FR-002 ("recorded on the original committed decision").
+- Durability: the event store is append-only; the record is never rewritten, so repeated retries read the same value (SC-001/SC-003), including after the execution advanced (the classifier scans full history, not the head).
+- Untrusted-input rule: repository files (events) are untrusted input; the matching code trusts the *validated* fields, and this change reuses exactly those validated fields rather than parsing caller JSON again.
+
+**Alternatives considered**:
+- *(a) Echo `request_body["signal"]["id"]` from the retry* — equal by construction once recognition passes, but it sources the answer from untrusted request data instead of the committed decision; if the classifier ever loosens, the reply could disclose a value the store never accepted. Rejected.
+- *(b) Read it off the current status snapshot / projection* — wrong source by design: later events may have advanced state; FR-002 explicitly forbids deriving it from current state. Rejected.
+- *(c) Second history scan inside `reply_with_current_status` to find the event at `original_decision_sequence`* — correct but costs an extra pass and duplicates matching logic the classifier already ran; rejected on cost/simplicity (Keel: reuse the proven symbol).
+
+## R2 — Placement of the identifier in the reply
+
+**Decision**: Inside the recognized-retry proof object: `data.idempotency.signalId`, present **only** when the command is `execution.signal` and the key classified as `Complete`.
+
+**Rationale**: The spec's placement default (recorded assumption) — the proof is "this reply proves a retry of that decision", so anything the proof attests belongs there. The status snapshot is shared with plain reads (`GET /v1/executions/{id}` via `execution::status::execute`), which must not gain the field (FR-003); the fresh-signal success uses a bespoke `data` built in `execution::signal::execute` that already carries its own top-level `signalId`.
+
+**Alternatives considered**:
+- *Top-level `data.signalId` on the retry reply* — would make the retry shape look like a fresh success (misleading: no `decision`/`mayProposeMutation` recomputed) and risks leaking into the plain status reader if the value were injected before the shared render. Rejected.
+- *A nested `originalDecision.signalId` sub-object* — new shape where a flat additive field suffices; rejected.
+
+## R3 — Field name
+
+**Decision**: `signalId` (camelCase, same spelling as the fresh reply's `data.signalId`).
+
+**Rationale**: The input request names the missing value as the "`signalId` that the first reply carried"; reusing the exact name lets a client match `retry.data.idempotency.signalId == first.data.signalId` with zero mapping. Wire identifiers in this API are camelCase (`recognizedRetry`, `originalDecisionSequence`, `headSequence`); consistent.
+
+**Alternatives considered**: `originalSignalId` (redundant — the whole proof already says "original"); `signal.decisionId` (wrong level). Rejected.
+
+## R4 — Plumbing mechanics (which symbols change)
+
+**Decision**: Thread one `Option<String>` through the existing private classification pipeline:
+1. `classify_one_key(...)` → widen the `Present` result with the captured identifier (`None` for every non-signal kind; for signals, taken from the matched `SignalRecorded` payload — the very event `expected.matches(event)` just authenticated).
+2. `classify_existing_keys(...)` → capture it together with `original_decision_sequence` from the first matched decision key (same event whose sequence is already carried).
+3. `KeyState::Complete` variant → carry `(sequence, Option<String>)`.
+4. `reply_with_current_status` / `reply_with_status_value` / `annotate_recognized_retry` → accept the optional value; insert `"signalId"` into the `idempotency` JSON object only when `Some`.
+5. Update the two internal call sites of the widened private fns (pre-flight arm, post-append conflict arm, and the private 500-response unit test at ~line 2776).
+
+**Rationale**: Single pass over already-loaded history; no extra store open; each widening is mechanical and keeps behavior provably identical for non-signal commands because `MutationDecisionKind::for_command` maps only `"execution.signal"` to `SignalRecorded` (`serve/mod.rs:740-756`). FR-007 holds *by construction*, not by per-command checks.
+
+**Alternatives considered**:
+- *New helper fn `signal_id_for(execution, sequence)` scanning history in `reply_with_*`* — extra scan + duplicate of the match/authenticate logic; rejected.
+- *Store the identifier in a side map keyed by sequence in `MutationIdentity`* — identity currently carries nothing about stored payloads (it's the caller-side proof identity); polluting it would blur the fail-closed design ("nothing from a mismatched event is returned"); rejected.
+- *Match on request body instead of payload in `classify_one_key`* — see R1(a). Rejected.
+
+## R5 — Which existing tests freeze the pre-change proof shape
+
+**Decision** (verified by repo-wide search for `originalDecisionSequence` / `recognizedRetry`): exactly two tests pin the exact shape of a **signal** retry proof, plus two internal call sites of the signature being widened:
+
+| Item | Location | Effect of the change |
+|---|---|---|
+| `a_retried_mutation_with_the_same_idempotency_key_appends_nothing` | `apps/cli/tests/api_http.rs` ~line 2187 | Must be updated to expect the enriched proof (spec names this update as part of delivery, not coverage relaxation). |
+| Private race cell asserting the recognized loser's exact `idempotency` object | `apps/cli/src/commands/serve/mod.rs` ~line 2961 (test uses `execution.signal`) | Same update; expected `signalId` read from the committed event payload. |
+| `a_non_object_retry_status_is_a_serialized_structured_500_response` | `serve/mod.rs` ~line 2776 | Signature-only update (annotation fails before the field matters). |
+
+Unaffected (verified): `sweep` (~5304) and `wake-lease` (~5341) retry cells assert only `recognizedRetry == true`; `runtime_http.rs:1745` prose references 409 behavior; refusal tests assert `idempotency` is `null` (still true post-change); `POST /…/signal` fresh-success assertions check `data.signalId` presence, unchanged.
+
+## R6 — RED→GREEN evidence protocol for the new regression assertion (SC-004)
+
+**Decision**:
+1. Add the new regression test to `apps/cli/tests/api_http.rs` **first** (fresh submit capturing `first.data.signalId`; byte-identical resend asserting `retry.data.idempotency.signalId == first.data.signalId`; a second resend asserting the same value again; a plain `GET /v1/executions/{id}` asserting no `idempotency` object).
+2. Run it against the pre-change tree and record the failing run (expected: `data.idempotency.signalId` is `Null` ≠ the expected string).
+3. Implement the source change; rerun: green.
+4. Keep the red record in the PR/evidence; a later green never erases the earlier red (JPD rule).
+
+**Rationale**: The field does not exist pre-change, so any assertion on it fails deterministically — a genuine red, not a flaky or environment-induced one. Running against a live spawned `graphhelm serve` in a tempdir (the suite's existing harness) makes the observation end-to-end over real HTTP, as FR-008 demands.
+
+**Alternatives considered**: asserting only in the private serve-layer unit tests (faster but not "the existing HTTP tests… end to end" as FR-008 words it); property-testing the id (overkill; value equality + repeat stability already covers the observable contract). Rejected in favor of the single focused HTTP cell.
+
+## R7 — Validation surface & minimal commands (offline, pinned toolchain)
+
+**Decision**: Validate with the smallest observers that reach each promise, all offline on Windows PowerShell with `cargo +1.97.1 … --locked`:
+- Targeted integration cells: `cargo +1.97.1 test -p graphhelm-cli --test api_http --locked -- <filtered test names>` (new regression test; updated exact-shape test; mismatched-actor refusal; wrong-kind refusal).
+- Targeted unit cells: `cargo +1.97.1 test -p graphhelm-cli --lib --locked -- commands::serve` (race cell, 500 cell, classification tests).
+- No workspace-wide build/full suite for this task per the unattended-run instruction; full `ci/gate.ps1` remains the eventual delivery evidence if/when a merge happens.
+
+**Rationale**: `api_http.rs` spawns the compiled `graphhelm serve` binary (`assert_cmd::cargo::cargo_bin!`), so building `-p graphhelm-cli` is the minimum compile unit that observes the behavior end to end; filtered test runs avoid unrelated suites.
+
