+# Feature Specification: Signal Identity on Recognized Retries
+
+**Feature Branch**: `001-bench`
+
+**Created**: 2026-10-27
+
+**Status**: Draft
+
+**Input**: User description: "`POST /v1/executions/{id}/signal` answers a recognised idempotent retry (same key, same body, same actor) with the current status and an `idempotency` block, but without the `signalId` that the first reply carried, so a client retrying after a lost acknowledgement cannot match it (`apps/cli/src/commands/serve/mod.rs`, `reply_with_current_status`). Return the original durable `signalId` on a recognised retry of `execution.signal`; a mismatched actor or a wrong-kind event must still get no retry proof and no `signalId`. Add the regression assertion to the existing HTTP tests in `apps/cli/tests/api_http.rs`; it must fail without your change."
+
+## User Scenarios & Testing *(mandatory)*
+
+### User Story 1 - Recover the original signal identity from a lost-acknowledgement retry (Priority: P1)
+
+A client submits a signal to an execution through the Runtime API, attaching a stable idempotency key so the submission is safe to repeat. When the acknowledgement is lost — a timeout, a dropped connection, a client restart — the client resends the very same request: same key, same body, same acting agent. The Runtime recognises the resubmission as a retry of a decision that already committed, and answers with the current execution status plus a retry proof that names the original decision. Today that proof does not carry the signal's identifier, although the first reply did. Without it, the client cannot match the signal it sent against the committed record: it cannot confirm *which* signal landed, correlate the signal's evidence, or de-duplicate follow-up work keyed on the signal identity.
+
+**Why this priority**: This is the core of the feature and the entire user-visible value: a lost-acknowledgement retry becomes lossless for signal identity. Every other story is a guardrail that keeps this change from widening what the reply discloses.
+
+**Independent Test**: Submit one signal over the signal endpoint, resend the identical request, and verify the retry reply carries the exact signal identifier the first reply carried. This delivers the complete user-visible value on its own.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** a signal was committed by agent A under idempotency key K, and the client never kept the first reply, **When** agent A resends the identical request (same key K, same body) to the signal endpoint, **Then** the reply succeeds, carries the recognized-retry proof naming the original decision, AND carries the original durable signal identifier — byte-identical to what the first reply returned.
+2. **Given** the original signal decision committed earlier and the execution has since advanced through further events, **When** the client retries the original request, **Then** the reply carries the original decision's signal identifier — not anything derived from current state — while still reporting the current status.
+3. **Given** a committed signal decision, **When** the client retries the request several times across intervals, **Then** every recognized-retry reply carries the same original signal identifier (durable and stable across repeats).
+
+---
+
+### User Story 2 - A mismatched actor still receives no retry proof and no signal identity (Priority: P2)
+
+An actor attribution is durable, not display metadata. Two agents can submit byte-identical bodies under the same idempotency key, but the second agent did not perform the first agent's decision. The new identifier must not create a channel through which the mismatched actor learns about the original decision that the refusal already hides.
+
+**Why this priority**: The feature adds data to a reply that sits next to security-sensitive refusal paths; keeping those paths fail-closed is part of the contract, second only to the happy path.
+
+**Independent Test**: Commit a signal under a key by agent A, resend the same key and body as agent B, and verify the refusal is exactly what it is today — no retry proof, no signal identifier, no new disclosure.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** agent A committed a signal under key K, **When** agent B submits the identical body under key K, **Then** the request is refused with the existing conflict refusal, the reply carries no retry proof and no signal identifier, and the refusal discloses nothing new about the original decision (in particular not the original actor).
+2. **Given** the refusal above, **When** the execution's event history is inspected, **Then** the refused retry appended nothing.
+
+---
+
+### User Story 3 - A wrong-kind committed event still receives no retry proof and no signal identity (Priority: P3)
+
+The event store holds untrusted input: a structurally valid event can sit under a reused key with the wrong decision kind. Key equality alone never authenticates a retry, and the new identifier follows the same rule — it travels with the retry proof and nowhere else.
+
+**Why this priority**: The same fail-closed principle as Story 2, applied to the other recognition dimension (decision kind instead of actor).
+
+**Independent Test**: Commit a structurally valid event of the wrong decision kind under the caller's key, present a signal request under that key, and verify the refusal carries no retry proof and no signal identifier.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** the caller's key already resolves to a committed event of a different decision kind, **When** the caller presents a signal request under that key, **Then** the request is refused with the existing conflict refusal, carrying no retry proof and no signal identifier, and nothing is appended.
+
+---
+
+### Edge Cases
+
+- **Fresh submissions are unchanged.** A first-time request under a key still returns its own signal identifier and no retry proof; the proof — with or without the identifier — appears only on recognized retries.
+- **Other commands' retries are unchanged.** Every mutation command other than the signal command keeps exactly today's retry proof (the recognized-retry flag and the original decision sequence, and nothing else). No other command has a signal identity to report, and this change must not widen their surface.
+- **No partial disclosure on refusal.** A divergent-key refusal (mismatched actor or wrong decision kind) carries no identifier even though the Runtime knows the original decision exists; it discloses no more than it does today.
+- **The identifier comes from the committed decision, not from current state.** Later signals may have been committed after the original decision; the retry still reports the original decision's identifier.
+- **Recognition rules are unchanged.** What qualifies as a recognized retry (same key, identical body, same actor, matching decision kind) is exactly as today; this feature only changes what a recognized retry of the signal command *answers*.
+- **Existing tests that froze the old proof shape.** Two existing automated tests assert the exact shape of a signal retry proof (recognized-retry flag plus original decision sequence, nothing else). They must be updated as part of this change to expect the enriched shape; that update is part of delivering the feature, not a relaxation of coverage. The NEW regression assertion must, separately, fail on the pre-change behavior.
+
+## Requirements *(mandatory)*
+
+### Functional Requirements
+
+- **FR-001**: The system MUST include, in the successful reply to a recognized retry of the signal endpoint, the original durable signal identifier — the value the first reply for that committed decision returned.
+- **FR-002**: The identifier MUST be the one recorded on the original committed decision: stable across repeats of the retry and unaffected by any state advancement since the decision.
+- **FR-003**: The identifier MUST travel inside the recognized-retry proof of the reply, so that any client that gates on the proof necessarily receives the identifier with it; it MUST NOT appear in the reply to a plain status read or to a fresh submission.
+- **FR-004**: When the same idempotency key and identical body are presented by a different authenticated actor, the system MUST answer with the existing fail-closed conflict refusal carrying no retry proof and no signal identifier, and MUST disclose nothing new about the original decision.
+- **FR-005**: When the caller's key resolves to a committed event of a different decision kind, the system MUST answer with the existing fail-closed conflict refusal carrying no retry proof and no signal identifier.
+- **FR-006**: A fresh (first) submission of a signal MUST continue to return exactly what it returns today: its signal identifier and no retry proof.
+- **FR-007**: Recognized retries of every mutation command other than the signal command MUST remain observationally identical to today's replies: the recognized-retry flag and the original decision sequence, and no new field.
+- **FR-008**: The change MUST be covered by a regression assertion added to the existing HTTP integration tests for the API that observes the retry reply end to end, and the assertion MUST fail when run against the pre-change behavior.
+
+### Key Entities
+
+- **Signal decision record**: The durable, immutable record created once when a signal is admitted and committed. Attributes: the signal identifier, the owning execution, the source (its kind and identity), the signal kind, its severity, and the digest of the signal envelope. Created exactly once per committed decision; never rewritten.
+- **Recognized-retry proof**: An additive, per-reply annotation stating that this request was recognized as a retry of an already-committed decision rather than re-executed. Attributes: the recognized-retry flag, the sequence of the original decision, and — new, and present for signal retries only — the original signal identifier.
+
+## Success Criteria *(mandatory)*
+
+### Measurable Outcomes
+
+- **SC-001**: 100% of recognized retries of a committed signal carry the original signal identifier, byte-identical to the value the first reply carried — including retries made after the execution state has advanced and retries repeated across intervals.
+- **SC-002**: 100% of divergent refusals (mismatched actor, wrong decision kind) carry no retry proof and no signal identifier; zero new disclosures on the refusal path.
+- **SC-003**: No observable regressions elsewhere: fresh submissions and recognized retries of all other commands behave exactly as before the change (all pre-existing automated acceptance tests still pass, with the two exact-shape signal-retry proofs updated to the documented enriched shape).
+- **SC-004**: The regression assertion demonstrates red-to-green: it fails in one observed run against the pre-change behavior and passes against the changed behavior.
+
+## Assumptions
+
+- **Constitution substitution.** No constitution file exists in this checkout; per the stage setup, the `AGENTS.md` file at the repository root is used as the governance reference wherever the spec-kit flow references `/memory/constitution.md`.
+- **"Original durable signal identifier"** means the identifier recorded on the committed signal decision, which is the same value the first reply returned. By construction of recognition, a recognized signal retry already implies the committed decision carries that identifier (the decision-kind match verifies the envelope's identity against the stored record), so reading it introduces no new failure mode.
+- **Placement default.** The identifier goes inside the recognized-retry proof of the reply (the one place scoped to "this reply proves a retry of that decision"), not at the top level of the status snapshot — because the same status snapshot is served by plain reads, which must not gain the field. This is an informed default resolving an ambiguity in the request; it changes nothing outside the proof.
+- **Scope boundary.** Only the signal command's recognized-retry reply changes. The rules that decide whether a request IS a recognized retry (key equality plus identical body, actor, and decision kind) are untouched, as are all refusal paths, all other commands, and the CLI face.
+- **Test location.** The regression assertion is added to the existing HTTP integration tests for the API (`apps/cli/tests/api_http.rs`), as requested; no new test file is introduced.
+- **Contract surface.** No checked-in documentation file pins the retry-proof shape; the observable contract is defined by the code and by the tests that freeze it. The two tests that freeze the old signal-retry proof shape are updated by this change (see Edge Cases); no other contract artifact needs editing.
+- **Unattended run.** Every ambiguity was resolved by an informed default recorded above; no clarification questions were left open.
